WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:35.000
This past year, technology has been the glue that's kept many of our relationships alive. Birthday parties and business meetings moved to Zoom, and we kept up with friends and acquaintances who we could no longer see in person with Facebook updates, FaceTime, and group text messages. Now, even as life begins to return to normal, technology-mediated communication is here to stay. Although offices are starting to reopen, many workplaces plan to allow teleworking to continue, which will mean those dreaded Zoom meetings are not. going away. But even before the pandemic, we were already relying heavily on social media to
过去这一年，科技成为了维系我们许多人际关系的纽带。生日派对和商务会议搬到了Zoom上，我们通过Facebook动态、FaceTime和群聊短信与无法亲身见面的朋友和熟人保持联系。现在，即使生活开始回归正常，这种以科技为媒介的交流方式也将长期存在。尽管办公室开始重新开放，但许多工作场所计划继续允许远程办公，这意味着那些令人头疼的Zoom会议并不会消失。但即使在疫情之前，我们就已经非常依赖社交媒体来

2
00:00:35.000 --> 00:01:21.000
keep us connected. Facebook alone has 2.8 billion monthly active users, and 85% of Americans now own a smartphone. More and more, when we talk to each other, we do it with some kind of technology between us. So what does this mean for human communication? Is the version of ourselves that we present on social media, authentic and truthful? Are people more likely to lie online or in a text message than they are in person, to video calls exhaust us more than in-person conversations do, and perhaps more broadly, is all of this technology-driven communication good for our mental health and well-being or not? Welcome to Speaking of Psychology, the flagship podcast of the American Psychological Association that examines the links between psychological science and everyday life.
保持联系了。仅Facebook每月就有28亿活跃用户，如今85%的美国人拥有智能手机。我们彼此交谈时，越来越多地通过某种科技手段进行。那么，这对人类交流意味着什么？我们在社交媒体上展示的那个版本的自己是真实可信的吗？人们在网上或短信中撒谎的可能性是否比面对面交流时更高？视频通话是否比面对面交谈更让我们疲惫？更广泛地说，所有这些由科技驱动的交流对我们的心理健康和幸福感究竟是有益还是有害？欢迎收听《心理学谈话》，这是美国心理学会的旗舰播客，旨在探讨心理科学与日常生活之间的联系。

3
00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:59.000
I'm Kim Mills. Our guest today is Dr. Jeff Hancock, a psychologist and professor in the Department of Communication at Stanford University. where he founded and directs the Stanford social media lab. Dr. Hancock is an expert in social media behavior and the psychology of online interaction. He is well known for his research on how people lie and whether we can detect deception in texts, emails, or in online reviews. His TED talk on deception has been viewed more than one million times. He's also studied how social media affects well-being and how artificial intelligence is changing the way we talk to one another. Thank you for joining us, Dr. Hancock.
我是金·米尔斯。我们今天的嘉宾是杰夫·汉考克博士，他是斯坦福大学传播系的心理学家和教授，并在那里创立并指导了斯坦福社交媒体实验室。汉考克博士是社交媒体行为和在线互动心理学方面的专家。他因研究人们如何撒谎以及我们是否能识破短信、电子邮件或在线评论中的欺骗行为而闻名。他关于欺骗的TED演讲观看次数已超过一百万次。他还研究了社交媒体如何影响幸福感，以及人工智能如何改变我们彼此交谈的方式。感谢您参加我们的节目，汉考克博士。

4
00:01:59.000 --> 00:02:29.000
Thanks, Kim. I'm excited to be here. So as I said, the name of your research group is the Stanford Social Media Lab. For a lot of people, when they think of social media, they think about Twitter or LinkedIn or Facebook or Instagram. You study those. But you also have a broader definition of what social media is. Can you explain that? Right, yeah. In fact, I had a social media lab at Cornell before there was social media, so speak. Because for me, social media is any technology that we use to be social with one another.
谢谢，金。很高兴来到这里。正如我所说，您的研究小组名为斯坦福社交媒体实验室。对很多人来说，提到社交媒体，他们会想到Twitter、LinkedIn、Facebook或Instagram。您研究这些，但您对社交媒体的定义似乎更广泛。能解释一下吗？没错，是的。事实上，在社交媒体出现之前，我就在康奈尔大学有一个社交媒体实验室了。因为对我来说，社交媒体是我们用来进行社交的任何技术。

5
00:02:29.000 --> 00:03:16.000
So I'm really interested in human-to-human communication when there's some technology between us. But even more broadly, you know, if you're talking to a robot in a way, I think of that as social media. When you're interacting with your car, that's a form of social media. And you and I here on a video conference, to me, is social media as well. So let's talk about something that falls under that broader umbrella, which is video chat. Your research into Zoom fatigue has gotten quite a bit of recent media coverage. What exactly is Zoom fatigue and why should we find it so exhausting to just sit in front of a computer screen and talk to other people? Well, that's exactly the question that my colleague Jeremy Bounce and I had when the pandemic first started and us faculty members had all this extra time because we didn't have to commute.
所以我对人与人之间通过某种技术进行的交流非常感兴趣。但更广泛地说，如果你在和机器人对话，我认为这也属于社交媒体。当你与汽车互动时，那也是一种社交媒体。而你和我在这里进行视频会议，对我来说也是社交媒体。那么，让我们谈谈这个更广泛范畴下的一个话题，即视频聊天。您对“Zoom疲劳”的研究最近引起了不少媒体报道。究竟什么是Zoom疲劳？为什么仅仅坐在电脑屏幕前与他人交谈会让我们感到如此疲惫？嗯，这正是疫情刚开始时，我和我的同事杰里米·拜伦森提出的问题。当时我们这些教职员工因为不用通勤，多了很多额外的时间。

6
00:03:16.000 --> 00:03:46.000
We weren't going into the lab. Meetings were getting canceled left and right, but everything went to Zoom. And we found ourselves having these conversations, but why would this be so tiring? You know, we're here, relaxed. you know, we're in our homes. And so what we started to look at were the kind of differences between Zoom conferences and face-to-face. And looking back, there was, you know, a lot of work in the 90s. My colleague here at Stanford, Pam Hines had done work on video conferencing and why they might be more fatiguing.
我们不去实验室了，会议一个接一个地取消，但所有事情都转到了Zoom上。我们发现自己一直在进行这些对话，但为什么会这么累呢？你知道，我们在这里，很放松，在家里。所以我们开始研究Zoom会议与面对面交流之间的差异。回顾过去，90年代有很多相关研究。我在斯坦福的同事帕姆·海恩斯曾做过关于视频会议以及为什么它们可能更令人疲劳的研究。

7
00:03:46.000 --> 00:04:25.000
But it sort of research died out to some degree because it wasn't, you know, a huge deal. People weren't using video conferences all the time. It was there if you wanted it, but most people didn't use it. So what we started to talk about was this general feeling of kind of exhaustion or tiredness. Sometimes afterwards, you know, Jeremy would say, I just don't feel like doing anything afterwards. Or I would say, I don't want to talk to anybody after that. So we found some sort of consensus on this idea that maybe Zoom meetings aren't necessarily more fatiguing than face-to-face, but have some unique fatigue. we think it's related to some of the nonverbal dynamics.
但这类研究在某种程度上中断了，因为当时它并不是什么大不了的事。人们并没有一直使用视频会议。如果你想用它就在那里，但大多数人并不使用。所以我们开始讨论这种普遍的疲惫感。有时事后，杰里米会说：“我之后什么都不想做。”或者我会说：“那之后我不想和任何人说话。”所以我们对这个观点达成了一些共识：也许Zoom会议并不一定比面对面交流更令人疲劳，但它确实有一些独特的疲劳感。我们认为这与一些非语言动态有关。

8
00:04:25.000 --> 00:04:54.000
So Jeremy laid this out in one of his articles really nicely. The fact that your face right now is really close to mine. It's almost like at an intimate level. And that's sort of physiologically arousing. Either we're going to be, you know, come to blows or we're going to have a kiss, right? Like it's just, it's very unusual for us to be this close. When we're on with lots of people, even if I'm not speaking, it feels like a lot of people are staring at us, something that we've called hypergays. And to me, then, one of the biggest ones psychologically
杰里米在他的一篇文章中很好地阐述了这一点。你现在的脸离我非常近，几乎达到了亲密的程度。这在生理上会引起唤醒。我们要么会打架，要么会接吻，对吧？我们离得这么近是非常不寻常的。当我们和很多人在一起时，即使我不说话，也感觉很多人在盯着我们，我们称之为“过度凝视”。对我来说，心理上最大的一个因素是

9
00:04:54.000 --> 00:05:26.000
is the mirror. So right now I'm speaking to you. I can see myself. In fact, my image is as big as yours. And while we've seen ourselves in the mirror before, we've never really seen ourselves socially behaving. When we go into the bathroom and check ourselves out, it's usually just for appearance, but not for how we behave. This has a huge impact on us, I think, and is very tiring. And I've been trying to track myself just how much time I look at my image versus yours. And so it's hard to do this kind of introspection. But I'm guessing it's about half the time.
镜子。此刻我正在和你说话，我能看到我自己。事实上，我的图像和你的一样大。虽然我们以前在镜子里见过自己，但我们从未真正见过自己进行社交行为的样子。当我们去洗手间照镜子时，通常只是为了看外表，而不是看我们的行为。我认为这对我们影响巨大，而且非常累人。我一直在尝试追踪自己看自己图像的时间与看你图像的时间对比。进行这种内省很难，但我猜大约是一半的时间。

10
00:05:26.000 --> 00:05:46.000
My attention is drawn back to my image. So these are some of the examples of why Zoom can be so fatiguing. Let's turn for a moment to your research on deception and lying. Your TED talk that I mentioned a moment ago is called The Future of Lying. What is the future of lying? How will it be different from its past?
我的注意力被拉回到了自己的图像上。所以这些就是Zoom为何如此令人疲劳的一些例子。让我们转到您关于欺骗和撒谎的研究。我刚才提到的您的TED演讲题目是《撒谎的未来》。撒谎的未来是什么样的？它将如何与过去不同？

11
00:05:46.000 --> 00:06:40.000
That is a really great question. For me, I was interested in how technology could change how often we lie, and then how we sort of end up trusting other people. Kind of two sides of the same coin in a way. When we first started looking at it, we would ask people like, where do they think people would lie the most? And what we found was this sort of folk theory that as soon as you couldn't see the other person, as soon as you couldn't hear the other person, line would go up. And it's sort of this nonverbal cue idea that if I, if I can't see them, then they can lie more easily and therefore they will. And it's wrong, it turns out, on a whole bunch of levels. And I think the most recent work that I think is the best on deception detection in general is Tim Levine's and it's summarized in his book, duped. And what we find when it comes to technology is his ideas from duped work perfectly. So the first is that most of the time, most people don't want to lie, right?
这是一个非常好的问题。对我来说，我感兴趣的是科技如何改变我们撒谎的频率，以及我们最终如何信任他人。这在某种程度上是同一枚硬币的两面。当我们刚开始研究时，我们会问人们：他们认为人们在什么情况下最容易撒谎？我们发现了一种民间理论，即一旦你看不到对方，听不到对方，撒谎就会增加。这是一种非语言线索的观点，即如果我看不到他们，他们就能更容易地撒谎，因此他们就会撒谎。事实证明，这在很多层面上都是错误的。我认为目前关于欺骗检测最好的研究是蒂姆·莱文的，他的观点总结在《被骗》一书中。当我们谈到科技时，他书中的观点非常适用。首先，大多数时候，大多数人不想撒谎，对吧？

12
00:06:40.000 --> 00:07:10.000
the best option usually is to tell the truth. And sometimes we're in positions where that's uncomfortable or awkward or could be harmful for us. And that doesn't really change when we go into technology. And I should also clarify that I'm not talking about the Russian spies or the scam artist, you know, the Prince from Nigeria or any of those folks. Instead, I'm talking about people that we know, people that we have a tie with. And in that case, it looks like the psychology really drives line, not
最好的选择通常是说实话。有时我们处于不舒服、尴尬或可能对自己有害的境地。当我们使用科技时，这一点并没有真正改变。我还应该澄清，我说的不是俄罗斯间谍或诈骗犯，比如来自尼日利亚的王子之类的人。相反，我指的是我们认识的人，我们有联系的人。在这种情况下，看起来是心理驱动了撒谎，而不是

13
00:07:10.000 --> 00:07:55.000
the technology. So we found that people will be just as likely to lie in technologically needed places as others, except there was one big difference, and that is when the technology left a record. So in some of our earliest work, we found that people would lie the least in emails. And emails are one of these, like, really recorded ones. So if I send you an email, not only do you have a copy of the lie I just sent you, but there's also ones on servers and everything. So most of the time, psychology is what's going to drive whether someone lies or not, not the technology, but there are some features. So if there's a record, people tend to think about that a little bit. Another one is whether we're synchronous or not. So when I'm face to face with you,
技术。所以我们发现，人们在科技媒介环境下的撒谎可能性与其他环境下一样，除了一个重大区别，那就是当技术留下了记录时。在我们最早的一些研究中，我们发现人们在电子邮件中撒谎最少。电子邮件是那种真正有记录的媒介。如果我给你发一封电子邮件，不仅你有一份我刚才发给你的谎言的副本，服务器上也有，到处都有。所以大多数时候，心理才是驱动一个人是否撒谎的原因，而不是技术，但技术确实有一些特征。如果有记录，人们往往会多考虑一点。另一个因素是我们是否同步。所以当我面对面和你交流时，

14
00:07:55.000 --> 00:08:40.000
there's only so much I can pause. Like if I pause more than a second, it gets kind of weird and you start to worry that maybe I'm having a stroke or something. And so we have to think of things really fast and so line occurs more often synchronously. Technology can give us a bit of a break on that and sometimes we even see that people are more honest in those places. So I think the future of lying is one that will be driven primarily by psychology. So do we have the need to lies or other options? Also by some of these affordances of technology like will there be a record of this or not. And so that will just continue to evolve. Now in our latest work, what we're seeing is, is that people are getting really good at lying, even in recorded media.
我能停顿的时间是有限的。如果我停顿超过一秒，就会变得很奇怪，你可能会担心我是不是中风了之类的。所以我们必须很快地思考，因此同步交流中撒谎发生得更频繁。技术可以让我们在这方面稍微喘口气，有时我们甚至发现人们在那些媒介中更诚实。所以我认为撒谎的未来将主要由心理驱动。我们是否有撒谎的需要，或者是否有其他选择？同时也受到技术的一些可供性影响，比如这是否有记录。所以这将继续演变。现在在我们最新的研究中，我们看到人们即使在有记录的媒介中也变得非常擅长撒谎。

15
00:08:40.000 --> 00:09:08.000
And there, you just choose the kind of lies you can do. So, for example, a student could say to me, you know, Professor Hancock, my printer's not working. And that's an okay lie in an email, because the chance of me going to his place and checking out his printer is almost zero. But if he were to lie and say something about when he submitted something, right? And then there's a record that can check that, then he's going to be in trouble. Are there, have you done comparisons among various media?
在那里，你只是选择你可以撒的谎。例如，一个学生可以对我说：“汉考克教授，我的打印机坏了。”在电子邮件中这是一个可以接受的谎言，因为我去他家检查他打印机的可能性几乎为零。但如果他撒谎说关于他提交某样东西的时间，对吧？然后有记录可以核实，那他就会有麻烦。您是否对各种媒介进行过比较？

16
00:09:08.000 --> 00:09:36.000
Are we more likely to lie via text or in person or over the phone? I mean, how do we lie differently in different situations? Right, great question. So we've done a number of these different kinds of studies. We use diary studies where we get people to record them. We've looked at log records, lots of different ways. And what we end up finding is that text and email typically have fewer lies than, say, phone calls. And it's because of that record.
我们更容易通过短信、面对面还是电话撒谎？我的意思是，我们在不同情况下撒谎的方式有何不同？没错，好问题。我们做过许多这类不同的研究。我们使用日记研究，让人们记录下来。我们查看了日志记录，用了许多不同的方法。我们最终发现，短信和电子邮件通常比电话通话的谎言更少。这是因为有记录。

17
00:09:36.000 --> 00:10:16.000
It's because we're not forced to say things really quickly. And it's sort of been hard to talk about it lately because with disinformation, everybody just thinks that once you're on the internet, everything is a lie. And it's true that there is a lot of deception online, but I try to differentiate between what I call the known network, so people that we have a tie with, and the unknown network. So that could be, anyway, from the Russian spy to just somebody who's just somebody who's a known network. trying to troll me to someone trying to scam me. And it's difficult sometimes to differentiate those. So is there a lot of disinformation online? Yes, it's a real major problem. But is that happening
这是因为我们不需要被迫很快地回答。最近谈论这个问题有点困难，因为随着虚假信息的出现，每个人都认为一旦上了互联网，一切都是谎言。确实网上有很多欺骗行为，但我试图区分我所说的“已知网络”（即我们有联系的人）和“未知网络”。这可能包括从俄罗斯间谍到只是试图骚扰我或诈骗我的人。有时很难区分这些。那么网上有很多虚假信息吗？是的，这是一个非常严重的问题。但这种情况会发生在

18
00:10:16.000 --> 00:10:49.000
between, say, Jeff and Kim, who have a professional relationship? No, very unlikely. In those kind of situations, we see line is more likely to be done on the phone, so vocally, or face-to-face, than it will be in an email or text message. What's been most surprising to you, you while studying the impact of technology online? Well, it was definitely the fact that people were lying the least in email, for example, one of our first studies. I remember just I don't, you know, it was really strange. And then when we started thinking about it and thinking about this idea of the record,
比如杰夫和金之间吗？我们有职业关系。不会，非常不可能。在那种情况下，我们发现撒谎更有可能发生在电话里，也就是口头上，或者面对面，而不是在电子邮件或短信中。在研究科技对在线影响的过程中，什么最让您感到惊讶？嗯，绝对是人们在电子邮件中撒谎最少的事实，例如，这是我们最早的研究之一。我记得……我不知道，这真的很奇怪。然后当我们开始思考它，并想到这个“记录”的概念时，

19
00:10:49.000 --> 00:11:28.000
once you think of that, it's just, it's so obvious. But I think a lot of deception detection research up until the 2000s were really focused on nonverbal cues. You know, this is the influence of Paul Ekman. and his colleagues and students that we really focused on nonverbal things and it made it into the pop culture now it's pretty clear that there aren't any really strong pinocchio noses there's no one cue that will always tell you somebody's line so the fact that it happens you know online versus face-to-face the nonverbals are just less important and instead it's some of these affordances of online like the record that we leave behind
一旦你想到这一点，它就太显而易见了。但我认为2000年以前的许多欺骗检测研究确实集中在非语言线索上。你知道，这是保罗·埃克曼及其同事和学生的影响，我们确实专注于非语言的东西，它现在已经进入了流行文化。现在很清楚，并没有什么真正的“匹诺曹鼻子”，没有哪一个线索能总是告诉你某人在撒谎。所以它在网上还是面对面发生，非语言线索的重要性只是降低了，取而代之的是在线技术的一些可供性，比如我们留下的记录。

20
00:11:28.000 --> 00:12:29.000
There's this idea that we're all envious of each other these days, not because people necessarily lie on social media, but because they present the best, most idealized vision of themselves with touched-up selfies or elegant dinners or beautiful vacation photos rather than the day-to-day drudgery. Is the way that people present themselves on social media authentic, or is that not authentic? Is that a big lie? This is one of the biggest complaints people have about social media that it's all just like people showing their greatest stuff. And I guess I have two big responses to that. One is that when we talk to people that we've just gotten to know or we see on the street or we see, you know, back in the pre-air in the hallway, we would not talk about all the crappy things or boring things that happened about us. We would say, hey, I just went skiing this weekend or I did that. that is we're always presenting a version of ourselves. And I don't think that's inauthentic.
现在有一种观点认为我们都在嫉妒彼此，不是因为人们在社交媒体上撒谎，而是因为他们展示了自己最好、最理想的一面，比如修图后的自拍、优雅的晚餐或美丽的度假照片，而不是日常的琐碎。人们在社交媒体上展示自己的方式是真实的，还是不真实的？这是一个弥天大谎吗？这是人们对社交媒体最大的抱怨之一，即人们只是在展示他们最棒的东西。我想对此我有两个主要的回答。第一，当我们和刚认识的人交谈，或者在街上看到，或者在疫情前在走廊里遇到时，我们也不会谈论所有发生在我们身上的糟糕或无聊的事情。我们会说：“嘿，我这个周末去滑雪了”或者“我做了那个”。也就是说，我们总是在展示一个版本的自己。我不认为这是不真实的。

21
